\f0\fs40 \cf2 Warm compresses and lid massage are used to help the meibomian glands release oil more easily. These glands sit along the eyelid margins and help slow tear evaporation. When the oil becomes thick or blocked, the eyes may feel dry, irritated, watery, or tired.\
\
Heat should be as warm as can comfortably be tolerated (test to ensure you do not burn yourself). A clean heat mask is often the easiest option. Apply it over closed eyes for about 5 to 10 minutes. The goal is to soften the thickened oil inside the glands. If you\'92re short on time, or for maintenance, continuously flowing warm water over closed eyelids (e.g. in the shower) may be used for ~60 seconds.\
\
After heating, gently massage the lids. For the upper lid, use a clean fingertip or cotton tip and roll downward toward the lashes. For the lower lid, roll upward toward the lashes. Use light pressure. The goal is to guide the oil toward the gland openings, not to rub hard. A few passes along each lid is usually enough.\
\
This treatment is usually done once or twice daily at first, then less often for maintenance depending on symptoms and your doctor\'92s advice. Consistency matters more than force.\
\
Important tips:\
